The Colonel shall be responsible for the management and control of the State Police. The Colonel shall make and adopt rules and regulations for the direction, control, discipline and conduct of the members of the State Police and such other rules for the government and operation of the State Police as he may deem necessary. He shall also designate the authority and responsibility for each rank of State troopers in the State Police. The Colonel shall also:

(a) Issue all medalions for Taxi Operators, and;

(b) Issue all permits for Commercial Aircraft Operators, and;

(c) Issue all permits for Commercial Drivers.

The Lee County Sheriff’s Office must provide protective services to the following individuals

(a) Justices of the Supreme Court;

(b) District Court Judges;

(c) Department Heads of County Departments that are on the County Government team;

No individual shall be permitted entry into the Lee County Jail unless they have been authorized by a Correctional Officer and one of the following applies:

(a) They are visiting an inmate, and the inmate has consented to the visit;

(b) They are a legal representative of an inmate;

(c) They are a law enforcement officer who has either been requested by Correctional Officers or is responding to an emergency situation;

(d) They are an emergency medical services provider, firefighter, or other emergency responder who was requested to respond by Correctional Officers; or

(e) They are otherwise authorized by a Correctional Officer.

Civilian Correctional Officers may, when on duty and in accordance with departmental guidelines:

(a) Detain any individual for up to twelve (12) minutes, or until the arrival of a sworn law enforcement officer, whichever occurs first, if the individual fails to comply with a lawful order to leave the Lee County Jail; commits or is suspected of committing a criminal offense (violations of Title 1 of the M.S.C.) within the grounds of the Lee County Jail or immediately adjacent thereto; commits a criminal offense against, within the vicinity of, or in connection with a prisoner work operation or community service operation supervised by Correctional Officers; commits or is suspected of committing a violation of 1 M.S.C. § 1311 Escape anywhere within the State; or if instructed to do so by a sworn law enforcement officer. In addition to the authority described above, a Civilian Correctional Officer may only detain an individual outside of the county jail grounds or prisoner work operations when it is immediately necessary to prevent death or serious bodily injury, no other reasonable alternatives exist, and sworn law enforcement officers are not immediately available. Civilian Correctional Officers must immediately notify and transfer custody to a sworn law enforcement officer as soon as practicable after detaining any individual under this subsection;

(b) Carry on their person and utilize, in accordance with provisions of State law the following articles of equipment and their respective ammunition or otherwise essential components; extendable, fixed and straight batons, stun guns or taser devices, pepper-spray or other similar chemical self-defense items, encrypted handheld radios, fixed or chain connected handcuffs; and department issued firearms;

(c) If authorized to do so by a sworn law enforcement officer close or block public roadways, establish, maintain, and assist in the operation of checkpoints and otherwise aid in the public service of the State;

(d) Use a reasonable amount of force to overcome resistance, prevent evasion, and facilitate their lawful duties;

(i) Civilian Correctional Officers shall not initiate or engage in vehicular pursuits unless they are passengers in a vehicle operated by a sworn law enforcement officer.

(e) Search any person, aircraft, or vehicle which seeks access to or is within the Lee County Jail.

(f) Perform any other reasonable duties or functions at the discretion of the Warden.

A person shall register under this subchapter if the person was charged with a felony violation, including a completed violation, attempted violation, or an act of aiding, abetting, or conspiring to commit any of the following offenses, and was convicted of or adjudicated guilty for that offense or for another offense arising from the same set of circumstances:

(a) Terrorism under 1 M.S.C. § 1104;

(b) Treason under 1 M.S.C. § 1105;

(c) Insurrection under 1 M.S.C. § 1106;

(d) Aircraft hijacking under 1 M.S.C. § 1811;

(e) First-degree murder under 1 M.S.C. § 2101;

(f) Second-degree murder under 1 M.S.C. § 2102;

(g) Voluntary manslaughter under 1 M.S.C. § 2103;

(h) Involuntary manslaughter under 1 M.S.C. § 2104;

(i) Vehicular homicide under 1 M.S.C. § 2105;

(j) Kidnapping under 1 M.S.C. § 2201;

(k) False imprisonment under 1 M.S.C. § 2202;

(l) Hostage Taking under 1 M.S.C. § 2203;

(m) Attempted murder under 1 M.S.C. § 2301;

(n) Assault with a deadly weapon under 1 M.S.C. § 2302;

(o) Aggravated battery under 1 M.S.C. § 2303;

(p) Hijacking under 1 M.S.C. § 2309;

(q) Armed robbery under 1 M.S.C. § 3101;

(r) Robbery under 1 M.S.C. § 3102;

(s) Arson under 1 M.S.C. § 3202;

(t) Aggravated stalking under 1 M.S.C. § 4109;

(u) Engagement in organized crime under 1 M.S.C. § 5101;

(v) Racketeering under 1 M.S.C. § 5202;

(w) Unauthorized possession of a destructive device under 1 M.S.C. § 6101;

(x) Discharging of a destructive device causing death or bodily harm under 1 M.S.C. § 6301; or

(y) Discharging of a destructive device under 1 M.S.C. § 6303.

Upon the declaration of a state of civil disorder, the Attorney General may:

(a) Mobilize all available state, county, or local law enforcement resources;

(b) Establish temporary curfews, restricted zones, and checkpoints as reasonably necessary to restore order;

(c) Deputize temporary peace officers for the duration of the declared emergency;

(d) Issue binding orders to subordinate peace officers and deputized personnel regarding enforcement priorities during the emergency.

The Director of the International Intelligence Agency is authorized to—

(a) collect intelligence through human sources and by other appropriate means outside the State of Mayflower;

(b) provide overall direction for and coordination of the collection of national intelligence outside the State of Mayflower through human sources by elements of the intelligence community authorized to undertake such collection;

(c) correlate and evaluate intelligence related to state security;

(d) provide appropriate dissemination of such intelligence to authorized recipients, including the Governor, heads of departments and agencies of the executive branch, the Adjutant General, military generals, and the State Senate and the committees thereof;

(e) collect (including through clandestine means), process, analyze, produce, and disseminate signals intelligence information and data for foreign intelligence and counterintelligence purposes to support national and departmental missions;

(f) establish priorities for signals intelligence activities carried out by elements of the intelligence community;

(g) conduct electronic surveillance, physical searches, and other acquisitions for foreign intelligence purposes, subject to court orders where required;

(h) conduct counterintelligence activities in support of signals intelligence operations;

(i) conduct counterintelligence activities outside the State of Mayflower and coordinate counterintelligence activities conducted outside the State of Mayflower by other agencies;

(j) support military operations and other national security efforts through the provision of signals intelligence;

(k) coordinate with other elements of the intelligence community on signals intelligence matters;

(l) protect the security of Agency installations, activities, information, property, and employees by appropriate means, including such investigations of applicants, employees, contractors, and other persons with similar associations with the Agency as are necessary;

(m) terminate the employment of any officer or employee of the Agency for International Intelligence whenever the Director deems the termination of employment of such officer or employee necessary or advisable in the interests of the State of Mayflower; and

(n) perform such other functions and duties related to intelligence affecting the national security as the Governor may direct.

(o) Subject to the approval of the Governor and consistent with applicable law, the Director of the Agency for International Intelligence is authorized to conduct special activities, including covert actions intended to influence political, economic, or military conditions abroad, where it is intended that the role of the State of Mayflower will not be apparent or acknowledged publicly, except that no such activity may be conducted for the purpose of influencing domestic Mayflower political processes, public opinion, policies, or media.

To provide compensation for assets, informants, non-public employees, and asylum seekers to advance the foreign interests of the State, the Director may–

(a) In concert and with the approval of the Director of the Law Enforcement Training Institute, provide for expedited or direct entry into a Law Enforcement Training Institute Class;

(b) In concert and with the approval of the Mayflower State Bar, provide for a transfer of equivalent certification into the State of Mayflower;

(c) In concert and with the approval of a Department Head, provide for expedited or direct entry into a public service department; and

(d) In concert and with the approval of a Department Head, provide for a promotion, transfer, or reassignment.

The State Archivist shall appoint the following officers to assist in fulfilling Commission duties:

(a) Assistant State Archivist for the Executive Branch;

(b) Assistant State Archivist for the Legilsative Branch;

(c) Assistant State Archivist for the Judiciary Branch;

The State Archivist may classify any archive or item as “Restricted” if:

(a) Its disclosure poses a threat to the national or governmental security of the State;

(b) The item is under investigation for fraud, falsification, or suspected unreliability;

(c) The information is strictly private and such determination is made by the State Archivist; or

(d) The Governor or Attorney General submits a written request stating justification, and the State Archivist approves such justification.

In this subtitle, a Military Installation means any of the following properties below:

(a) Fort Standish within the Military Exclusion Zone;

(b) Lander City Berginson Armory;

(c) Winchester Recruitment Center; and

(d) Camp Bryant.

Members of the Mayflower National Guard shall be free from civil and criminal liability for violations of the Mayflower Vehicle Code when—

(a) Part of a convoy when a member of the Military Police is participating;

(b) During an activation or official operation of the guard;

(c) Taking part in a training exercise authorized by a Senior Officer; or

(d) When a State of Emergency has been declared.

The Staff Judge Advocate, under the direction of the Adjutant General, shall:

(a) Perform duties relating to legal matters arising in the Mayflower Army National Guard as may be assigned to him or her;

(b) Establish minimum requirements for prospective candidates to become judge advocates, including pay grade and benefits, with the consent of the Adjutant General;

(c) Either oversee or appoint in his name an officer or officers to oversee the various functions, as given by the Adjutant General, of the Judge Advocate General’s Corps; and

(d) Perform such other duties as may be assigned to him.

Engineers shall be given permanent jurisdiction within the state which their services and assistance for visible civil works missions that include—

(a) planning, designing, building and operating locks and dams, which includes flood control, beach nourishment and dredging for waterway navigation;

(b) design and construct fortifications and peace lines within civilian areas when deemed necessary to ensure public safety;

(c) design and construct checkpoints, roadblocks or any other form of temporary traffic control system at the request of law enforcement officers with the relevant authority to conduct such operations; and

(d) environmental regulation and ecosystem restoration.

The Honor Guard shall be authorized to perform the following duties:

(a) Performing ceremonial functions at state and military events;

(b) Escorting protectees as designated by the Adjutant General or his designee;

(c) Representing the State of Mayflower in intergovernmental or commemorative events;

(d) Protecting designated sites, as defined by 3 M.S.C. 4 § 1121;

(e) The governor may issue ceremonial orders expanding or modifying the scope of authorized events.

The Honor Guard shall be vested with the authority to establish a five-stud ceremonial protection zone surrounding the following, as necessary for the fulfillment of its ceremonial duties:

(a) The perimeter of any designated ceremonial site during an Honor Guard Operation;

(b) Any protectee actively under Honor Guard escort;

The Adjutant General, or his designee, shall be obligated to establish operation regulations including, but not limited to:

(a) Uniform and conduct standards;

(b) Parade Fire regulations;

(c) Protocols for coordination with security services;

In this subtitle, ‘Port Authority Installation’ refers to any of the properties below:

(a) The Lander Marine Terminal;

(b) The International Commerce Center;

(c) Any bridge and tunnel within the State of Mayflower;

(d) The Standish Tunnel Command.

The Office of the Inspector General shall be established as the independent investigative and auditing agency for all Port Authority personnel and internal operations.

(a) The Office of Inspector General shall have authority to conduct administrative investigations, audits, and compliance reviews;

(b) The Office of Inspector General may issue reports, refer findings for disciplinary or criminal prosecution, and recommend policy changes;

(c) The Office of Inspector General shall have unrestricted access to all documents, systems and personnel under the Port Authority’s jurisdiction;

(d) The Commissioner, or their designee, may establish internal policies that impose restrictions or limitations on the operations, personnel, or investigative scope of the Office of Inspector General, provided such policies do not obstruct ongoing investigations or compromise the Office’s statutory independence.

The Tunnel and Bridge Commission shall be obligated to perform the following duties:

(a) The Commission shall maintain trained firefighting and rescue personnel;

(b) The Commission shall ensure readiness of firefighting apparatus and emergency vehicles at all crossings;

(c) The Commission shall coordinate with local fire departments in mutual aid operations;

(d) The Commission may impose safety regulations and inspection protocols specific to Port Authority Installations, as defined in 3 M.S.C. 5 § 1110.

Port Authority Public Safety shall:

(a) Port Authority Public Safety shall provide security to properties and assets maintained fully or in part by or under the purview of the Port Authority, as defined in 3 M.S.C. 5 § 1110;

(b) Not hold peace office status nor be classified as private security officers. However, shall:

(i) Be granted the powers, authorities and privileges afforded to private security officers under the Security Services Act. This includes but is not limited to being designated as an ‘authorized officer’;

The Attorney General or his designee, upon conclusion of the investigation, shall either determine no violation was made, or submit a notice to the violator of the violations under this Chapter they sustained. Such notice will include a full description of the violation, and the corrective action taken, which may include:

(a) referral to the internal investigative unit of the agency which the violator is a member of;

(b) a fine of no more than $10,000; or

(c) removal from state service or a reduction in grade or rank;

(i) removal or reduction may only take place after the filing of an administrative action on behalf of the State against the violator.

The Governing Council shall be composed of:

(a) the Chancellor, who shall be an ex officio member;

(b) one member appointed by the Chancellor among the alumni;

(c) two members, none of whom shall be students, members of the administrative staff or members of the teaching staff, appointed by the Governor; and

(d) three members elected by the administrative staff among the administrative staff.

The government, management and control of the University, and of the property, revenues, business and affairs thereof, are vested in the Governing Council, and, without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the Governing Council has power to:

(a) appoint, promote, suspend and remove members of the teaching and administrative staffs of the University, as the Governing Council considers necessary, but no member of the teaching staff shall be appointed, promoted, suspended or removed unless recommended by the Chancellor or such other employee of the University designated therefor by the Chancellor under this section;

(b) fix the number, the duties and the salaries of the University;

(c) delegate such of its powers under clauses i and ii to the Chancellor as it considers necessary;

(d) provide for the granting of and grant degrees, including honorary degrees, diplomas and certificates;

(i) the Chancellor shall be provided the privilege of granting or revoking degrees to any resident of the State;

(e) borrow from time to time real property for the use of the University, and give such security against the assets of the University by way of mortgage or otherwise, as it determines;

(f) determine and regulate the standards for the admission of students to the University, the contents and curricula of all programs and courses of study and the requirements for graduation;

(g) delegate such of its powers under clauses iv, v and vi as it considers proper to the Chancellor; and

(h) do all such acts and things as are necessary or expedient for the conduct of its affairs and the affairs of the University.

The duties of a peace officer shall include the following but is not limited to:

(a) preserving the peace;

(b) preventing crimes and other offenses;

(c) assisting victims of crime;

(d) apprehending criminals and other offenders who may lawfully be taken into custody;

(e) participating in prosecutions;

(f) executing warrants that are to be executed by peace officers and performing related duties;

(g) performing lawful duties as promulgated by their respective department head or the Attorney General;

(h) completing training required by this act, the peace officer’s respective department, or a law enforcement training institute; and

(i) performing such other duties as assigned to the peace officer by this act or any other Act, including any prescribed duties.

A peace officer shall not use force unless:

(a) the force is used for the purpose of carrying out a lawful duty;

(b) the peace officer is entitled by statute or common law to use force for the purpose of carrying out that duty;

(c) the peace officer is acting on reasonable grounds; and

(d) the force used is no more than is necessary given the circumstances.

No person shall be hired, carry out the duties, or act in the capacity of a peace officer unless he or she:

(b) is physically and mentally able to perform the duties of the position;

(c) does not have any pending criminal matter before any court of the State or the Union of Columbia in which the person is named as the defendant and has been reasonably notified of such matter;

(d) has not been convicted of any degree of misconduct within the last fifty (50) days;

(e) holds an active certification by a recognized law enforcement training institute; and

(f) is actively employed by a recognized law enforcement entity within the State for the purposes of keeping the peace.

Every department or railroad company that employs at least one peace officer is required to establish an internal investigation unit that is staffed with at least one peace officer who shall not be in any other division or department office. The duties of this unit shall include, but are not limited to, the following:

(a) Primary duties include, but are not limited to:

(i) ensuring employed peace officers are compliant with department policies;

(ii) ensuring employed peace officers are compliant with regulations issued by the Attorney General;

(iii) maintaining confidentiality where appropriate;

(iv) conducting impartial investigations regarding contraventions of department policies, this act, and any regulations prescribed under this act; and

(v) recommend re-education training or disciplinary measures to the department head.

(b) From time to time, issue a publicly accessible document or message regarding disciplinary measures taken within the respective department since the last publication.

(c) Forward the result and any relevant documentation to the Attorney General upon conclusion of any investigation.

(d) Ensure that an accessible method for reporting police misconduct is available to the public.

The department head of a law enforcement agency is responsible for:

(a) ensuring department policies and procedures are in compliance with all applicable laws and regulations;

(b) overseeing and managing all activities of their respective department;

(c) in writing, authorizing police operations in their respective department; and

(d) approving all offers of employment of their respective department.

A peace officer is not required to identify themselves if:

(a) it is not reasonably safe for the peace officer;

(b) the peace officer is operating an emergency service vehicle;

(c) the peace officer has already identified themselves to the same person within the last sixty (60) minutes;

(d) the peace officer has declared themselves out of service; or

(e) the peace officer is engaged in or participating in an authorized police operation where the act of providing identification would compromise the objective(s) of the operation.

The term “automatic firearm” shall be defined as;

(a) Any self-loading firearm capable of continuously firing projectiles when the trigger mechanism is activated.

(b) Any self-loading firearm capable of firing multiple projectiles per a single activation of the trigger mechanism.

Any individual in possession of a firearm with a modification made to, affixed to, or installed on that serves any of the following purposes shall be guilty of 1 M.S.C. § 6102: Unlicensed Possession of a Firearm;

(a) Barrel attachments or other modifications which suppress or otherwise silence the audible noise generated by the process of discharging a projectile.

(b) Modification to reduce the length of a firearms barrel to be less than three (3) studs in length, or to reduce the length of the firearm entirely to be less than six (6) studs.

All Government run departments and agencies under the executive branch(es) of government, including law enforcement agencies, within the state shall be obligated to create and publish a public database, that is easily accessible within a separate public documents or similarly titled channel within a department’s communication server, and has:

(a) a registry of employees and each employees:

(i) assigned unit/division;

(ii) current position;

(iii) rank;

(iv) callsign/badge number; and

(v) complete disciplinary history;

(b) a code of conduct with all punitive titles as defined by 4 M.S.C. 7 § 1003;

(c) a list of all discharges; and

(d) a list of all administrative actions.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class I’, or any other similar name

(a) Items, kits, or modifications tools that can be used to modify an automatic firearm;

(b) Items, kits, or modification tools which can cause a firearm that is not an automatic firearm to become so;

(c) Law enforcement exclusive equipment.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class II', or any other similar name:

(a) Items, kits, or modifications tools that can be used to modify a firearm;

(b) Crates containing illegal firearms;

(c) Crates containing illegal ammunition.

(d) Gavels; and

(e) Items which show membership, admittance or allegiance to a criminal organization or enterprise.

The following items shall collectively be known as ‘Prohibited Goods, Class III’, or any other similar name:

(a) Gold Bars;

(b) Silver Bars;

(c) Dirty Money;

(d) Encrypted Phone;

(e) Military Encryption Cards; and

(f) File of Military Intelligence.

(g) Underground Firearms Access Card.

(h) Lockpicks.

“Money Laundering” shall be created and defined as follows:

(a) Any person, knowing that the property involved in a financial transaction represents the proceeds of some form of unlawful activity, conducts or attempts to conduct such a financial transaction which in fact involves the proceeds of unlawful activity with the intent to promote the carrying on of specified unlawful activity; or knowing that the transaction is designed in whole or in part;

(i) to conceal or disguise the nature, the location, the source, the ownership, or the control of the proceeds of specified unlawful activity; or

(ii) to avoid a transaction reporting requirement under Local, State or Federal law;

(b) Shall be guilty of a felony and sentenced to 30 minutes in prison and/or face a fine of not more than $5,000 or twice the value of the property involved in the transaction, whichever is greater.

(c) This statue shall be set to active within the Mayflower Criminal Code.

An Insurance Business owner must satisfy the following requirments to be permitted to conduct business as an Insurance Business:

(a) Have a minimum capital of the result of the following formula:

(i) (n*C)+(C*3);

(ii) Where n is the maximum amount of persons the Insurance Business is going to insure; and

(iii) Where C is the maximum Claim amount that an Insurance Business is going to compensate for.

(b) Provide the Commerce Office a prospective business report that includes, in the order as listed:

(i) The names of all stakeholders in the business;

(ii) The names of any stakeholders in the business that have owned an Insurance Business which has been insolvent.

(iii) The maximum amount of Policyholders the Insurance Business is to insure;

(iv) The maximum Claim the Insurance Business is to insure for;

(v) The total capital the owner of the Insurance Business possesses; and

(vi) Plans on how to pay out Policyholders in case of insolvency.

(c) If an Insurance Business desires to change the maximum Claim or maximum Policyholders after registration, then the Insurance Business must submit a new prospective business report

Insurance Businesses must clearly outline all types of Insurance Policies and its individual rules provided by the Insurance Business in a clear and discernable method, which is publicly accessible, which includes:

(a) How the Insurance Policy applies to the Policyholder;

(b) The Premium of the Insurance Policy;

(c) The term of coverage that the Policyholder receives for the Insurance Policy; and

(d) An example on how the Insurance Policy will compensate the Policyholder.

Every month, if a Policyholder submits a Claim to the Insurance Business, the Insurance Business must publish a summary claims report which is publicly accessible that includes:

(a) The total amount of Claims submitted by Policyholders;

(b) The total amount of Claims accepted by the Insurance Business;

(c) The total amount of Claims rejected by the Insurance Business, with their reason(s) for rejection;

(d) The total amount of Policyholders, including the difference from the previous public report;

(e) Any changes to an Insurance Policy;

(f) Any loans that the Insurance Business has obtained to finance the Insurance Business, and the amount of money the loan is worth;

(g) Any court convictions against the Insurance Business;

(h) Any commercial punishments against the Insurance Business by the Commerce Office; and

(i) A link to the prospective business report.

An Insurance Business may only reject to compensate for a Claim submitted by a Policyholder where:

(a) The Policyholder misrepresents the facts in a Claim;

(b) The Policyholder fails to pay Premiums;

(c) The Policyholder requests the Claim as a result of their involvement in criminal activity;

(d) The Policyholder attempts to submit a Claim for something that is not covered by the Insurance Policy;

“Insurance Fraud” shall be defined as:

(a) Any Policyholder that fraudulently submits a Claim to an Insurance Business;

(b) Shall be guilty of a felony and sentenced to 25 minutes in prison, and/or face a fine of not more than $30,000 or four times the value of the Claim, whichever is greater.

Transportation Service Companies shall be required to maintain a publicly accessible database of their drivers, including but not limited to the following:

(a) the driver's Roblox username;

(b) the driver's date of hire;

(c) the driver's position;

(d) the driver's registered vehicle model; and

(e) the license plate of the driver's registered vehicle model.

Arbitrary punishment is defined as:

(a) punishment that has no backing in regulation, policy, or law;

(b) punishment whose policy, law, or regulation prescribing it is ambiguous, or unclear, or a violation of a constitutional right or privilege or statutory enactment;

(c) punishment with no factual backing;

(d) punishment outside and beyond what is explicitly prescribed in policy; or

(e) punishment that takes no accounting of mitigating factors, circumstances, or liabilities.

When made aware of a violation of this Subchapter, the Attorney General or his designee may issue an order:

(a) Suspending the employment of a violator until they are in compliance with this Subchapter, or;

(b) Terminating the employment of a violator for egregious violations of this Subchapter done knowingly.

The attributes of the merit system shall be as follows:

(a) When making decisions of employment or promotion, officials shall review the following attributes of merit:

(i) logged patrol/work hours for the current cycle;

(ii) past performance in other departments;

(iii) abilities to work as a team and play well with others, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader or employee;

(iv) demonstration of sound judgment, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader;

(v) demonstration of leadership qualities while holding a position in the State of Mayflower, which is affirmed by testimony of another leader or employee;

(vi) effort in the completion of an application, cover letter, or letter of interest;

(vii) scores on an accredited exam, if applicable;

(viii) commendations or written letters of recommendation; and

(ix) holding a valid accreditation, which may be required for the position.

(x) Completion of formal state training or continued education relevant to the position.

When making a hiring decision officials can use the following attributes to discredit and demerit a hire:

(a) bad performance in another department or agency;

(b) poor completion of an application, cover letter, or letter of interest;

(c) poor accredited exam scores;

(d) past disciplinary actions;

(e) character defects which would bring harm to the health of the department or community;

(f) failure to use discretion;

(g) inability to gain a security clearance, other than not requiring it in the first place;

(h) refusal to comply with regulations or lawful orders;

(i) terrible activity in a previous department or agency; or

(j) no, or little experience within the State of Mayflower.

It shall be an unlawful employment practice for an agency or department to create, enforce, or maintain any policy, directive, or order that prohibits, restricts, or penalizes any employee from wearing their hair of choice, unless the employee’s hair of choice:

(a) Isn't either black, brown, blonde, ash, gray, white, or any other natural hair color;

(b) Is long enough to the point it reaches the legs;

(c) Extends out horizontally by over 2 studs from the head.

For the purposes of this section, a “state holiday” shall refer to:

(a) The week of Christmas, which is on the 25th Day of December;

(b) The week of New Year’s Eve, which is on the 31st of December;

(c) The week of American Thanksgiving, which is on the fourth Thursday of November;

(d) The week of Easter, which is on the first Sunday after the full moon, which occurs on or after the spring equinox.

(i) If the full moon falls on a Sunday, then the Easter holiday shall be on the next Sunday.

(e) The Sixth (6th) day of August shall be known as Tayvion Cole Rememberance Day, in honor of the great Bloxveldo-Columbian football player and rapper who was taken from this world far too soon.

(f) The fifth day of July (07/05) shall be known as Happy Day, as a way to improve the morale and happiness of all Mayflower citizens.

After being searched, any individual may freely access:

(a) The main atrium of the State Capitol Building;

(b) Any room of legislative purpose that is not being utilized for official business, or when it is not within 10 minutes before such a room is to be used for official business;

(c) Both floors of the Mayflower Supreme Court when not in use, and when it is not within 10 minutes before the Mayflower Supreme Court is to be used for official business;

(d) The waiting room of the Executive Floor of the State Capitol.

A provisional license may be granted for legitimate purposes, including but not limited to:

(a) Participation in a recognized degree course as established under 4 M.S.C. 4 § 1208(d).

(b) Participation in a recognized legal traineeship or apprenticeship program by a certified law firm;

(c) Temporary legal practice by out-of-state or foreign attorneys pending their full admission to the State Bar of Mayflower;

(d) Completion of practical training requirements mandated by the State Bar for full licensure.

In consultation of its committee members, the State Bar may also adopt additional rules, regulations and procedures necessary for:

(a) Disciplining, suspending, or disbarring attorneys who violate professional standards or commit acts of misconduct;

(b) Administering and enforcing legal education requirements to maintain attorney competency and ethical practice;

(c) Resolving disputes between attorneys and their clients, including fee arbitration.

(d) All rules adopted by the State Bar are binding on members upon adoption and must be made publicly available. The Bar may amend or repeal rules as necessary.

Before adopting, amending, or repealing any rule, the State Bar shall:

(a) Provide notice of the proposed rule to all members and the public, specifying changes and inviting written comments;

(b) Consult with members if deemed necessary;

(c) Submit the proposed rule to the Supreme Court for final approval before it takes effect.

(d) All rules become effective once enacted and must be published in a way that is accessible to all members and the public.

The levy must be used strictly to fund the administration and operation of the State Bar, including:

(a) regulatory activities;

(b) member services;

(c) educational programs;

(d) public protection initiatives;

(e) pro bono programs to assist indigent populations;

(f) reappropriation of funds into the Administrative Office of the State of Mayflower Courts;

(g) failure to pay the levy as prescribed may result in penalties, including suspension of membership; and

